om friends and mentors.\n  - these kids: Caroline is excited to build a family and give a loving, safe home to 'these kids' through adoption, ensuring they experience love and acceptance.\n  - unity and strength: Caroline painted 'unity and strength' after visiting an LGBTQ center to capture the unity and strength of the people there.\n  - kids: Melanie's son was in a scary road trip accident but is now okay. Melanie mentioned her kids enjoyed an activity they did yesterday to relax after the trip. Caroline is passionate about helping kids through adoption.\n  - supportive community of hope: Caroline aims to build a strong, supportive community of hope by sharing her journey and experiences to promote understanding and acceptance.\n  - courage to transition: Caroline stated that realizing she could be herself without fear and having the courage to transition was the best part, calling it freeing to express herself authentically with support.\n  - LGBTQ advocacy stuff: Melanie asked Caroline about any upcoming exciting LGBTQ advocacy stuff after discussing Caroline's positive mentoring experiences.\n  - Showing off our true selves: Melanie stated that Caroline finding her true self is inspiring and that Caroline is now helping others.\n  - adoption: Caroline passed adoption agency interviews last Friday, viewing adoption as a way to build her family, give back, and show love and acceptance.\n  - apology: Caroline apologized to some people she got into a bad spot with while hiking last week, which Melanie commended as taking courage and maturity.\n  - references: Caroline mentioned that references are documents needed when preparing for adoption, alongside financial info and medical checks.\n  - love and support: Caroline is ready to offer love and support to others after achieving self-acceptance. She plans to build a family through adoption to give love and acceptance to kids in need.\n  - next time: Melanie asked Caroline what she hopes to do for the next time, fol